CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member-template deduction from const funptr arg: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*no body for callee.*$
--
N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/3: for a forwarding reference Fn&& and an lvalue
argument of type `const FP` (a const function pointer), Fn is deduced as
`const FP&`, so the deduced parameter `const FP&` binds a const function-pointer
lvalue.  The front end dropped the const while *re-converting* the deduced
reference's pointer base: cpp_convert_typet::read_rec's ID_pointer branch pushed
an already-converted (const) pointer to `other` without recording its top-level
cv-qualifiers, so the trailing c_qualifiers.write (is_constant=false) stripped
the const ([dcl.ptr], [basic.type.qualifier]).  The const-less parameter `FP&`
no longer bound the const argument, the sole template candidate was discarded
during overload resolution, and -- during instantiated-member-body elaboration
-- the call resolution silently failed (the "all candidates are templates"
SFINAE-tolerance path in cpp_typecheck_resolvet::resolve), leaving the enclosing
member with no body.

Fixed by recovering the pointer's own cv-qualifiers in read_rec's ID_pointer
branch.  This is the shape of libstdc++ std::function's
_Function_base::_Base_manager<F>::_M_manager __clone_functor case calling
_M_init_functor(__dest, *const_cast<const _Functor*>(...)); the fix makes a
single `std::function<int(int)> f = &fn;` no longer report _M_manager as
no-body.  Non-vacuous (the deliberately-wrong assertion must FAIL).

